.s which was agreed  already back in copenhagen 2009  for um poor people's adaptation to  climate change and poor people's help to  mitigate climate change for instance  into renewable energy's energy  efficiency okay rich countries have not  done their job dr stefan singer thanks  very much indeed  thank you  do stay with us coming up i'll be joined  by climate activist laura young and  michael jacobs professor of political  economy at the university of sheffield  and we'll discuss whether britain's  electric car industry really needs to  step up again  hello and welcome back to the daily  climate show on sky news now we're  getting straight to discussing the  climate issues of the day now with  climate activist laura young and  professor of political economy at the  university of sheffield michael jacobs  so good evening to both of you  so electric vehicles are a big part of  the government's plan to reach net zero  by 2050 and it's already announced that  the sale of new petrol and diesel cars  will be banned by 2030. however as we  heard earlier in the show the government  has been warned that it's falling behind  in its battery manufacturing industry  but how essential is that to our green  ambitions so michael the government  falling behind on its battery  manufacturing could that jeopardize the  uk's net zero targets  no i don't think so um we are going to  stop all new petrol and diesel cars from  2030  and that will mean a huge increase in  electric vehicles and those electric  vehicles will need batteries but if  they're not those batteries are not  manufactured in the uk that doesn't stop  the manufacturers installing them and  people buying them they will be supplied  from the rest of the world most of the  world's batteries are now produced about  70 percent in asia  some in europe the uk only has  one current uh battery manufacturing  plant the nissan plant in sunderland  there are plans for a few more um but if  they're not produced in the uk that's  not a problem for the net zero target  it's a problem for our economy and it's  our problem for the jobs that could be  created in green industries  this is the crucial point is that when  we go for  ambitious targets as we've got for net  zero we should be trying to maximize the  employment and exporting and industrial  and local regeneration opportunities  that arise from uh making the products  and the and providing the services and  that's where we're falling behind  do you agree with that laura potential  implications as around green jobs but  not necessarily our net zero targets  as michael has said we will find our  electric cars elsewhere but one of my  main concerns when it comes to the uk  and thinking about electric vehicles is  our reliance on using those as one of  the main ways to get to knees you know i  think one of the focuses that we need to  also be looking at is how we're going to  get to net zero through active and  public transport putting some of those  electric batteries into buses and really  investing in public transport  infrastructure as well i think that's  one of the flaws that i've seen is  always trying to think about how we can  get people from petrol cars into  electric cars versus thinking of how we  can get people using a variety of  different types of transport many of  which are better than electric cars  laura isn't one of the problems with  that is that you're trying to say to  people give up some of your freedoms  people like having their cars and the  freedom that it gives them doesn't that  turn people off the greener message if  you say no go and use public transport  instead  i don't think it's taking anything away  from people actually if anything the way  that our public transport is set up just  now it's really inaccessible to many  people whether that's price or whether  that's geography and so actually by  investing in public transport and active  travel we give people more choices i am  an individual who has an electric car  but i would like to use public transport  more but it's just not affordable or  it's just not in the right places i  would also love to use my bike more and  walk more but we just aren't set up for  that and we need to be looking at how we  give people more choices especially in  city centres where public transport  should be easy we see that in examples  like london but also seeing how we can  connect up different areas like rural  areas so that is an option that people  want to go for rather than always having  to opt for their car and michael the  government would say there are various  initiatives to try and improve the green  public transport around the country as  well but do you think the emphasis is  about right as far as  you know individual cars and public  transport is concerned and the whole lot  going green what do you think the  emphasis is about right  we have to do both of those things of  course we have to make the vehicles that  we do have electric and and not just  electric in their own propulsion but  that electricity being produced by  renewable energy sources which is  actually what makes the car green it's  not just the kind of electricity it's  not just having electric uh vehicles but  we also have to do as uh as you said the  the the walking the cycling the public  transport as well but i would emphasize  that this is a in both in both of these  areas this is a huge opportunity for the  uk  uh economy to go green for people to be  employed in green sectors for the jobs  that will be lost because we're not  going to have so much oil and gas  production um uh to be uh to be  recreated in for example making  batteries and i think it is very  disappointing that um after  really more than a decade of knowing  that the electric vehicle revolution was  coming um this was actually first  started under the labor government and i  was part of that process we first  identified the need for electric vehicle  battery  manufacturing plants  um in about 2008  and it's now 2022  and the uk still only has one okay we've  only got plans for a few more you know  there are 12 gigawatt hours of lithium  battery manufacturing plant planned in  the uk by 2025 12.  the germany expects to have 164 gigawatt  hours of plants the u.s okay 91.  okay i'm just going to i'm gonna i'm  gonna i'm just gonna stop that's gone  wrong in the way we've been playing okay  sorry to to speed you up there but i  just want to get onto the next subject  have a bit of time for that as well so  let's move on to that shall we because  as we mentioned earlier in the show the  hosts of this year's winter olympics  have called them the greenest games ever  but are winter sports actually  sustainable in a warming climate and as  locations with real snow become scarce  is their future on a slippery slope  michael the first winter olympics to  entire rou rely entirely on artificial  snow do you think the future of winter  games are in jeopardy  i certainly don't think we should ever  be having an olympic games that  requires the so the snow to be  manufactured um this is deeply  unsustainable um and uh this is not a i  think the criticisms that will be made  of china that are being made under the  international olympic committee will  mean that they won't uh be able to do  this again  um but this is going to be a problem for  all winter olympics the snow based um uh  tourism industry particularly in europe  is in decline because there is less snow  it's moving up the mountains away from  the resorts in many cases  which does mean that the future olympic  games will be uh in jeopardy i'm sure um  and this can't be solved simply by  trying to produce a  huge carbon cost uh fake snow so i do  think that there is going to be a  problem in the future  of all the problems however that we face  uh as a result of climate impacts i  don't think not having olympic games is  the greatest of them  although laura obviously that will come  as great sadness to people and and  individuals who enjoy skiing and  snowboarding obviously it's not  everybody it's some people will consider  it a bit of a luxury anyway but they'll  really worry about future holidays as  well is it something that you think we  should be concerned about  i think we should be concerned about  everything that's going to be impacted  by the climate crisis and the one thing  that i am pleased about if i can phrase  it that way is that this is getting  attention and it's raising awareness to  the fact that even something like the  olympics the winter olympics and snow  sports are going to be impacted by  climate change and really i hope it  makes us look at not just snow but  actually the olympics as a whole as a  process as something that happens every  four years how we can make that more  sustainable and really raise awareness  of all the areas that are going to be  impacted by this and even if you don't  ski it's still nice to be able to go out  on a snowy day and see something that  should be natural in many places around  the world and i think this will  hopefully get us moving even quicker  towards the sustainable future that we  need so that we can enjoy skiing on the  slopes so laura do you think this whole  conversation actually is good for  raising awareness then as you're saying  i mean that the fact that people will  think  be surprised perhaps to know that  they're going to be fewer and fewer  possible locations in the future for the  winter olympics they may have to travel  further for  for a winter skiing holiday at least it  raises awareness of the issues  i think it is really important and  actually artificial snow has been used  before in winter olympics not 100 of it  but even as far back as the 1980s we  were using it to top up our winter  olympic sports and i think it's now that  we realize because our awareness has  been raised through many other things  that we are starting to pay attention  and there's been studies done in the  last four years and even some research  that came out just this year looking at  how many previous locations wouldn't be  viable to be used for the winter  olympics because of safety and because  they wouldn't have any snow by 2050 and  even looking as far into the future as  20 80 if the temperatures keep rising at  the way they are going and i think this  is something that has really put  sustainability on center stage looking  at it through a lens of sport and this  is the kind of interdisciplinary  approach that we need to take when it  comes to climate action looking at how  it's going to affect everything and  making people really sit up and realize  that this is going to impact them and  all the things that they enjoy so we  need to get moving okay on that note we  will leave it but laura young and  michael jacobs thank you both very much  indeed for your time  and that's it for me but we'll be back  next time with more climate news every  weekday at 8 30 here on the daily  climate show so thanks for watching i'll  see you next time  [Music]  you
 

